+## Native file drags
+
+`domicile-native-drag-events` streams this desktop's native drag lifecycle as
+JSON lines. Spawn the fixed command with `domicile.processes.spawn` and consume
+its stdout through the returned process handle.
+
+- The first line is `{"kind":"ready"}` after the compositor accepts the
+  subscription.
+- `{"kind":"native_drag","app_id":"..."}` identifies the source of a
+  client drag whose implicit pointer-grab serial Smithay has validated.
+- `{"kind":"native_drag","app_id":null}` marks pointer-button drop
+  completion. It does not report that the destination accepted the data.
+- A shell tracks its native `<app>` pointer presses. On a matching drag start,
+  it releases that element's DOM pointer capture so motion can hit-test other
+  native windows. Other presses keep their capture.
+- The launcher pins the helper to its matching binaries and supplies
+  `DOMICILE_NATIVE_DRAG_SOCKET` for that desktop. The helper refuses a missing
+  or relative socket, messages over 4096 bytes, and unexpected protocol frames.
+- Startup has a five-second deadline. After readiness, the helper waits for
+  lifecycle events without polling. A lost compositor connection is an error;
+  restart the subscription on `compositorreplaced` and stop it on shell cleanup.
+- Event delivery uses the compositor's outbound writer. The Wayland input
+  thread queues each event without waiting for a subscriber.
+
 ## Application dialogs
 
 Applications ask for dialogs through `xdg-desktop-portal`, and the compositor
